  "market_context": "Live walkthrough on XAUUSD 15-minute chart during active trading hours (Fri 23–Mon 26 May). Price ranged between ~3260–3370. Analyst demonstrated a repeatable RSI-based entry method without requiring divergence confirmation.",
  "key_observation": "The analyst observed that RSI re-entering the 70/30 zone after exiting it (overbought/oversold) consistently signaled momentum shifts, and vertical lines placed at those re-entry points mapped to high-probability long/short entries on price.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst identified the RSI default settings (period 14) on the XAUUSD 15-minute chart and emphasized waiting for RSI to leave the 70/30 zone.",
    "Step 2: He then waited for RSI to re-enter the 70/30 zone and marked the first point of re-entry (a local peak or trough inside the zone) with a circle.",
    "Step 3: Using the vertical line tool, he placed a vertical line at each circled re-entry point on the RSI subchart.",
    "Step 4: He mapped those vertical lines back to the price chart to identify exact entry levels for long or short positions.",
    "Step 5: For trade execution, he advised checking left-side market structure and placing stop loss below the previous swing low (for longs) or above swing high (for shorts).",
    "Step 6: He noted that divergences were present ~90% of the time but were not required—entries were based solely on RSI re-entry timing."
  ],
  "outcome": "Approximately 7 wins out of 9 signals; one loss went in direction but stopped out, another loss occurred. Overall ~80% win rate claimed.",
  "lessons": "RSI re-entry into the 70/30 zone is a simpler, higher-probability trigger than waiting for divergences. Patience for the exit-and-re-entry sequence improves timing, and confluence with market structure increases reliability.",
